PRIVATE REFLECTION: {
Current leaning: 23. The literal wording asks for 95% confidence that the Nagar relative asymptotic bias is below 10%, not a Stock–Yogo table lookup for a different criterion. For just-identified TSLS with one endogenous regressor and one instrument, relative bias ≈ 1/μ², so need μ² > 10. First-stage F ~ noncentral χ²₁(μ²). The one-sided 95% critical value is the 95th percentile of χ²₁(10): (√10 + 1.645)² ≈ 23.1, rounding to 23.

Round 2 public opinion strongly favors 23: Colleagues 1, 10, 11, 12, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 8, 9 support 23. Dissent: Colleagues 14, 15, 7 still favor 16 via Stock–Yogo 16.38. Colleague 13 proposes 22 using a Taylor/normal approximation F ≈ N(1+μ², 2+4μ²), giving 11 + 1.645√42 ≈ 21.66. The exact noncentral χ² calculation gives 23.1, so 22 is an approximation artifact.

Key argument against 16: 16.38 is the familiar Stock–Yogo critical value, but under the Nagar criterion it gives only about 81% confidence that μ² > 10; its implied 95% lower bound for μ² is about 5.77, corresponding to relative bias ~17.3%. Thus it does not satisfy the stated 95% confidence that relative bias < 10%, and it may be for maximal IV size rather than this bias confidence bound. Earlier Colleague 8’s initial 9 appears erroneous; they later switched to 23.

Unresolved issues: whether the question intended the canonical Stock–Yogo benchmark (possibly 16) despite the literal wording; and whether “95% confidence” should be interpreted one-sided (1.645, yielding 23) or two-sided (1.96, yielding about 26). Standard interpretation for “confidence that bias is less than 10%” is one-sided, so 23 remains best. Majority now favors 23.
}

