10.text:
PRIVATE REFLECTION: {
Current leaning: 23. The literal one-sided 95% confidence inversion of the Nagar relative-bias approximation gives \(\mu^2>10\) for relative bias <10%; with \(F\sim \chi^2_1(\mu^2)\), the 95th percentile at \(\mu^2=10\) is \((\sqrt{10}+1.645)^2\approx 23.1\), rounding to 23.

Majority in round 4 supports 23: C1, C11, C12, C15, C2, C3, C4, C5, C6, C8, C9. The 22 camp is C13, C14, and C7, who switched from 23 to 22. They argue the prompt explicitly permits Nagar/Taylor/asymptotic approximations, so using \(F\mid \mu^2=10 \approx N(11,42)\) gives \(11+1.645\sqrt{42}\approx 21.66\), rounding to 22. Rebuttal: the exact noncentral \(\chi^2_1(10)\) percentile is simple and more accurate; approximations are permitted but not required. So 22 is a defensible approximation route, but 23 better matches the literal 95% confidence inversion.

C10 remains with 16, using Stock–Yogo 16.38. The 23 camp argues 16.38 is the familiar weak-IV cutoff for a different calibration, likely maximal IV size, not the direct 95% lower-confidence bound for 10% relative asymptotic bias. Under exact noncentral inversion, \(F=16.38\) gives only about 81% confidence that \(\mu^2>10\); its 95% lower bound for \(\mu^2\) is about 5.77, implying relative bias around 17.3%. Colleague 15 also claims the Stock–Yogo 10% maximal-bias critical value is about 22.85, which rounds to 23. Unresolved: exact Stock–Yogo table interpretation, and whether the intended method is exact noncentral inversion vs. internally consistent asymptotic approximation.

My position: remain at 23. If forced onto the purely normal/Taylor approximation route, 22 is coherent, but the exact noncentral \(\chi^2_1\) calculation is elementary and directly implements the wording. The key contest remains 23 vs. 22; 16 should not displace them for the literal criterion.
}

